  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAs of 1 PM Friday afternoon, a deep trough remains across the \neastern CONUS with ridging across the high Plains and trough coming \nashore across the western US. At the surface, an expansive area of \nhigh pressure remains across eastern portions of KS, NE and into MO \nand IA. This is contributing to mostly sunny skies and temperatures \nin the 40s to near 50. The overall weather pattern will remain quiet \ninto Saturday with the surface high pressure remaining nearby. As \nsuch, temperatures into the 40s and 50s continuing with light \nwinds. The aforementioned trough axis is expected to weaken upon its \narrival to the central/southern Plains late Saturday into Sunday. \nEnsemble guidance remains consistent with the best rain chances \nremaining across western KS, far southern KS, and into OK. That \nbeing said, the long range HRRR and RAP solutions suggest the best \nrain chance may extend further into KS. This forecast remains \nlargely leveraged towards the global ensemble guidance with the best \nchances remaining across western and  far southern KS. Total rain \namounts are likely to remain less than 0.25\" for most locations. \nAreas near the OK border stand the best potential for rain totals \ngreater than 0.25\". \n\nTransitioning into next week, an active midlevel pattern may emerge \nwith multiple storm systems potentially impacting the central \nPlains. The first shortwave trough may arrive towards the middle of \nnext week with a second towards the following weekend. These storm \nsystem passages are likely to be accompanied by strong winds as \nwell. In fact, southerly winds may gust up to 45 mph Tuesday and \nWednesday with the highest wind gusts across central KS.",
